      }
    },
    "agreement": "Both harnesses, on different substrates, put the optimum at k=6 for all three inherited leagues, with monotonic degradation out to k=48. The finding is real."
  },
  "WHY_NOTHING_WAS_CHANGED": {
    "rule": "V9.3 F8, enforced by tests/test_ladder_replay.py::TestHyperparameterSweep",
    "quote": "ladder_replay.sweep's own docstring: 'DIAGNOSTIC ONLY... A constant chosen on this sample and then evaluated on this same sample produces an optimistic number... the approval decisions are NOT computed with swept values: they use the carried constants, which is also what the deployed model would actually run.'",
    "what_was_attempted_and_reverted": "SHRINK_GAMES was set to 6.0 in model_{epl,ligamx,laliga} and GOALS_ONLY_LADDER['M2']['shrink'] aligned to match. Two existing tests failed and were RIGHT to: aligning the ladder to a swept value makes the approval score itself on a constant chosen from its own evidence, which is exactly what F8 forbids. A parity test written alongside the change asserted the OPPOSITE of the existing rule. All reverted.",
    "the_legitimate_path": "k=6 needs OUT-OF-SAMPLE confirmation \u2014 the 2026-27 seasons as they accumulate \u2014 not a same-sample sweep. Until then the carried k=24 stands and the sweep remains what its author intended: a diagnostic that says what a re-sweep would buy, nothing more.",
    "what_the_diagnosis_still_explains": "The Liga MX symptom is unchanged and correctly understood: at k=24 with ~9 recency-weighted games each rating is ~72% prior, which is why Puebla and Guadalajara were served identical xG (1.343 vs 1.337) while the market had them 58.2/21.4. That gap is model under-confidence, NOT a mispriced market, and must not be read as value."
  }
